A look at the three approaches competing to help beta cell replacement survive the immune system
Issued on behalf of AVAI Bio, Inc.
LAS VEGAS, Sept. 08, 2026 (GLOBE NEWSWIRE) -- Biotech Insider News Commentary - Type 1 diabetes has an unusual property among serious diseases: the science of curing it is largely solved. Put working insulin-producing beta cells back into a person, and their body will begin to regulate glucose again. That is not a hypothesis anymore. It has been demonstrated in humans, repeatedly, with patients coming off insulin entirely. The obstacle is not whether replacement cells work. It is that the immune system destroys them, and the drugs that stop the immune system from doing so carry costs of their own.
