otc,otc:af6cc28857fd,2026-09-09,AUTR,"","",news,Autris - Saving Giant Sea Turtles,https://www.otcmarkets.com/stock/AUTR/news/Autris---Saving-Giant-Sea-Turtles?id=533753,false,"",4985,"AUTRIS AND VERITAS VILLAGES HELP PRESERVE ENDANGERED SEA TURTLES IN NICARAGUA FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE September 9, 2026, Panama City, Republic of Panama. - Autris (OTC: AUTR), (“Autris” or “the Company”), a pioneering company dedicated to promoting freedom and self -sustainability, through the acquisition, design, development, and construction of self-sustainable Veritas Village communities throughout Latin America and the Caribbean, announced details of its Giant S ea Turtle Sanctuary at Veritas Village – Playa Pacifica in Nicaragua, where the community has spent the past decade helping protect one of the world's oldest and most endangered species. Sea turtle eggs are most vulnerable to predators, poachers, and the elements during the incubation period before they hatch. The sanctuary at Playa Pacifica relocates eggs to a protected, monitored nesting area, allowing them to incubate safely through thi s vulnerable window before the hatchlings are released into the ocean. Over the past decade, the sanctuary has helped release thousands of hatchlings back into the wild. Many of the eggs cared for at the sanctuary are purchased directly from local “Hueveros” (Spanish), or “Egg Hunters”, who have traditionally sold sea turtle eggs on the local market, where they are considered a delicacy. By paying H ueveros more than they would otherwise receive, the Company redirects eggs that would likely have been sold for consumption into the sanctuary's conservation program, while supporting the livelihoods of the local families who collect them. There are four sea turtle species native to Nicaragua, and the sanctuary has cared for eggs and hatchlings from all four. The largest of the four species, the Giant L eatherback, have been found to weigh over 2,000 pounds and have a shell diameter of over 9 feet, although typical adults are considerably smaller. Sea turtles are known to return to nest on the same beach where they hatched once they reach reproductive age, a process that can "
